---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/iodine`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Iodine Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Iodine', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Iodine: Thyroid, Pregnancy, Salt Fortification and the Kelp Overdose Problem"
  description="Iodine reference — RDA, thyroid hormone synthesis, pregnancy requirements, salt-iodisation history, and why kelp supplements can push you into overdose territory."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/iodine", label: "Iodine" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Iodine</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Iodine's only known biological role is as a component of the thyroid hormones T3 and
      T4. Salt iodisation was one of the great 20th-century public health interventions —
      eliminating endemic goitre and preventing millions of cases of iodine-deficiency
      brain damage. Adequacy sits between too-little (goitre, cretinism) and too-much
      (thyroid dysfunction) in a narrower window than most people realise.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adults:</strong> 150 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 220 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 290 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 1,100 µg/day for adults — narrower for children.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pregnancy — the critical window</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Severe maternal iodine deficiency in early pregnancy causes irreversible cognitive
      impairment (endemic cretinism) in the fetus. Even mild-to-moderate deficiency
      correlates with lower child IQ. All prenatal vitamins should contain ~150 µg iodine;
      many don't — check the label. The American Thyroid Association recommends women
      planning pregnancy or breastfeeding supplement 150 µg/day of potassium iodide.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Goitre and hypothyroidism</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Chronic iodine deficiency causes the thyroid gland to enlarge (goitre) as it works
      harder to trap iodide. Severe deficiency causes hypothyroidism with fatigue, cold
      intolerance, weight gain, and dry skin. In children, it stunts growth and impairs
      neurodevelopment.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Iodine excess — the Wolff-Chaikoff and Jod-Basedow effects</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Acute large doses briefly shut down thyroid hormone production (Wolff-Chaikoff effect)
      — usually protective and self-limited. Chronic excess can either provoke autoimmune
      hypothyroidism (Hashimoto's) or, in people with underlying nodular thyroid disease,
      trigger hyperthyroidism (Jod-Basedow effect). Kelp supplements can contain 500–3,000+
      µg per serving; some batches wildly exceed the label claim. Don't stack kelp with
      iodised salt and a multivitamin.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Are you getting enough?</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Not automatically. The trend toward "sea salt," "pink Himalayan salt," and non-iodised
      "kosher salt" is quietly reversing decades of iodisation progress. Non-dairy milk
      alternatives, absent egg intake, and low seafood intake compound the problem.
      Australian, UK, and pregnant-women surveys have all documented rising mild iodine
      deficiency.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Seaweed, kelp (1 g dried): 232 µg (wildly variable — up to several mg)</li>
      <li>Cod (3 oz): 158 µg</li>
      <li>Iodised table salt (¼ tsp): 76 µg</li>
      <li>Milk (1 cup): 56 µg</li>
      <li>Yoghurt (1 cup): 75 µg</li>
      <li>Egg (1 large): 26 µg</li>
      <li>Shrimp (3 oz): 35 µg</li>
      <li>Tuna, canned (3 oz): 17 µg</li>
      <li>Prunes (5): 13 µg</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> If you have autoimmune thyroid disease (Hashimoto's
      or Graves'), sudden increases in iodine (from kelp, supplement stacks, or amiodarone)
      can worsen thyroid dysfunction — check with your endocrinologist before adding kelp.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/iodine" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
